+- **Whether core infra should live in dot-named repos at all.** Raised
+  2026-08-23, not decided, and deliberately not actioned by the session that
+  raised it. `add_repo` refuses any repository whose name begins with `.`, and a
+  *private* dot-named repo has no fallback — the anonymous lane serves public
+  repos only, so `.github-private` returns 403 on the API and on git alike.
+  `.github` is reachable purely because it is public, which is a property of its
+  visibility rather than a designed access path.
+
+  The reason this belongs in §7 and not in §5 is that it is not merely a trap to
+  route around. **The split is what caused §4.** `workflows.test.mjs` resolves
+  its workflow directory relative to its own checkout, so its blast radius is
+  exactly one repo; `.github-private` was never covered and the same defect
+  landed a third time. Porting the ratchet fixes the instance, not the class —
+  and the same shape recurs in `org/rulesets/ci-green.json` living apart from the
+  `schema` job it would gate.
+
+  Almost nothing in either repo is pinned there by GitHub: only `profile/`,
+  the fallback issue templates, and the community-health files. The 7 reusable
+  `workflow_call` workflows, the 2 composite actions, ~15 scheduled workflows,
+  the root tooling, and the whole registry are ordinary infra that could live in
+  normal repos — where they would also become SHA-pinnable, which
+  `uses: ./.github/actions/...` can never be. A fuller write-up with the
+  measurements is at
+  `bdelanghe/bdelanghe:handoff/dot-named-core-infra.md`. It needs an issue here;
+  the session that wrote it could not reach this repo to file one.
